sentences.t:
“Oidme, las escuelas, cue[m]des e ifançones!
cometer quiero un ruego a mio Çid el Campeador;
assí lo mande Cristus que sea a so pro.

sentences.l:
“Hear-me, the retinues, co[u]nts and infanzones!
to-put-forward I-want a request to my Çid the Campeador;
so it may-ordain Christ that it-be to his profit.

sentences.t:
Della e della parte, quantos que aquí son,
los mios e los vuestros que sean rogadores;
dándoslas, mio Çid, si vos vala el Criador!”

sentences.l:
From-that and from-that side, as-many-as that here are,
the mine and the yours that they-be petitioners;
give-us-them, my Çid, so you may-help the Creator!”

sentences.t:
Luego se levantaron iffantes de Carrión,
ban besar las manos al que en ora buena naçió;
camearon las espadas antel rey don Alfons.

sentences.l:
At-once themselves raised Infantes of Carrión,
they-go to-kiss the hands to-him who in hour good was-born;
they-exchanged the swords before-the king don Alfons.

sentences.t:
“Evad aquí vuestros fijos, quando vuestros yernos son;
de oy mas, sabed qué fer dellos, Campeador;
sírvanvos commo a padre e guárdenvos cum a señor.”

sentences.l:
“Behold here your sons, since your sons-in-law they-are;
from today more, know what to-do with-them, Campeador;
let-them-serve-you as a father and guard-you as a lord.”
